    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire was the product of one of medieval history's most remarkable careers of personal state-building, that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub</a>, known in the West as Saladin, a Kurdish commander of exceptional political intelligence who transformed himself from a constrained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">vizier</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a> into the master of the largest Muslim realm between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nile">Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tigris">Tigris</a>. Saladin's path to power began in March 1169, when his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>, the Zengid general who had taken control of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, died suddenly and Saladin was appointed vizier in his place. Two years later, as the last Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-adid">al-Adid</a> lay dying in September 1171, Saladin had the Friday sermon pronounced in the name of the Abbasid caliph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam-idea">Shi'a</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and returning Egypt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> allegiance; the Ayyubid state dates from this moment. His position was at first constrained by nominal subordination to the Zengid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a>, but Nur al-Din's death in May 1174 freed Saladin to pursue independent expansion. He took Damascus that autumn, progressively absorbed the Zengid principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> through a combination of military pressure and diplomacy, sent his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#turanshah">Turanshah</a> to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yemen">Yemen</a> in 1174, securing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#red-sea">Red Sea</a> route, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1183 and received the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mosul">Mosul</a> in 1186. The strategic logic of this long consolidation was explicit: Saladin was encircling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusader-states">Crusader states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#levant">Levant</a> before delivering the decisive blow. The ideological foundation of this project was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jihad">jihad</a>, above all the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from Christian rule, which gave Saladin's empire a purpose and a legitimacy that transcended the Kurdish origins of his family.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#cairo-citadel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel.jpg" alt="Cairo Citadel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_-_HuTect_ShOts_-_Citadel_of_Salah_El.Din_and_Masjid_Muhammad_Ali_%D9%82%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A9_%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%8A%D9%88%D8%A8%D9%8A_%D9%88%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF_%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%8A_-_Cairo_-_Egypt_-_17_04_2010_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ahmed Al.Badawy from Cairo, Egypt, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire governed the most economically productive region of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a>, spanning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, sustained by the annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nile">Nile</a> flood, and the commercial centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>, the Levantine coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>. Egypt's agricultural surplus, drawn from the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> and the irrigation networks of the Nile valley, financed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a>'s campaigns and the court culture of the Ayyubid sultans, and was distributed to commanders through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqta</a></em> system of revenue assignments. The period was a significant one for Egyptian commercial development: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a>, begun by Saladin in 1176 and expanded by his successors, served as both the seat of government and a symbol of the dynasty's investment in urban infrastructure, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> in particular promoted trade with the Italian maritime republics, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venice">Venice</a>, Pisa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genoa">Genoa</a> secured commercial privileges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexandria">Alexandria</a> that brought European goods into Egypt in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a>, alum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and textiles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#palestine">Syria-Palestine</a> corridor was one of the most commercially vital regions of the medieval world, connecting the overland routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acre">Acre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tyre">Tyre</a>. The Ayyubid tolerance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusader-states">Crusader</a> coastal enclaves, formalised in successive truces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa (1192)</a> onward, was partly a commercial calculation: the Frankish ports channelled Mediterranean trade on which Muslim merchants also depended. The empire's structural economic weakness was its fragmentation into semi-autonomous principalities, which divided revenues among competing princes and prevented the accumulation of resources for sustained campaigns against either the Crusaders or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-hattin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin.jpg" alt="Battle of Hattin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saladin_Guy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthew Paris, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid army was built on the Kurdish and Turkic warrior traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a>'s milieu, organised around a professional cavalry core of mamluks, slave soldiers mostly of Turkic origin, supplemented by Kurdish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#turkmen">Turkmen</a> contingents, Arab auxiliaries and the forces of subordinate princes. Saladin's genius was less tactical than strategic and psychological: he understood that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusader-states">Crusader states</a> could only be defeated by a unified Muslim army that denied them the chance to exploit divisions among their neighbours, and his patient consolidation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> was designed to create precisely that force. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187 was the culmination of this strategy: by drawing the Crusader army out from the springs of Sephoria into waterless terrain at the height of summer and denying it access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>, Saladin forced the entire field army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> to fight under extreme disadvantage. The victory was total: the army was destroyed, the relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#true-cross">True Cross</a> captured and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guy-of-lusignan">King Guy</a> of Lusignan taken prisoner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> surrendered on 2 October 1187. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> (1189–1192) revealed the limits of Ayyubid power against determined European opponents: Saladin lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acre">Acre</a> to the combined forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> and Philip II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> in 1191 and was defeated in the field at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-arsuf">Arsuf</a>, though he retained Jerusalem under the Treaty of Jaffa. Later Ayyubid military performance deteriorated as the structural problem of the system took hold: sultans spent disproportionate resources suppressing the ambitions of rival Ayyubid princes rather than confronting external enemies, and the growing reliance on mamluk regiments, above all al-Salih Ayyub's Bahriyya, created a professional military establishment that proved more durable and cohesive than the dynasty it served.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#damascus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damascus.jpg" alt="Damascus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damascus,_Syria,_Panoramic_view_of_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ayyubid government reflected a fundamental tension between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a>'s decision to distribute the empire's territories as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanages</a> among his kinsmen and the practical requirements of centralised rule. Saladin's logic was pragmatic: by giving his brothers, sons and nephews direct control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yemen">Yemen</a> and the Jazira, he secured their loyalty and their military contribution to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jihad">jihad</a> while retaining supremacy as sultan. The result was a decentralised family confederation, often described as semi-feudal, in which each prince maintained his own court, army and revenues while acknowledging the senior sultan's authority. Egypt, the economic heartland, was usually the senior ruler's direct domain and the principal source of the resources that sustained central authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a>, built on a spur of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muqattam-hill">Muqattam Hills</a> overlooking the city and continuously developed by Saladin's successors, became the administrative centre of Ayyubid Egypt and remains one of the most impressive surviving monuments of medieval Islamic rule. The Ayyubid sultans patronised religious scholarship, endowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madrasa">madrasas</a> that consolidated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> learning in former Fatimid Egypt, and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-aqsa-mosque">al-Aqsa Mosque</a> to Muslim worship after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-jerusalem-1187">reconquest of Jerusalem</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> jurisprudence predominated at the Ayyubid court, though the dynasty also endowed institutions for the other Sunni schools, and the sultans cultivated the religious legitimacy essential to their role as defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> and protectors of the holy places. The critical failure was the absence of any accepted mechanism of succession: there was no rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, and the death of a senior sultan repeatedly triggered conflict among his heirs that drained the dynasty's military and financial resources.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerusalem.jpg" alt="Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Mount_(Aerial_view,_2007)_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire's historical legacy is dominated by the towering figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-jerusalem-1187">reconquest of Jerusalem</a> in 1187 and relatively merciful treatment of its Christian inhabitants, contrasting sharply with the massacre that accompanied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-crusade">First Crusade</a>'s capture of the city in 1099, created one of the medieval world's most enduring legends of noble warfare. Saladin's reputation transcended religious boundaries: Christian writers celebrated him as a model of chivalric virtue even though he had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from them, and his exchanges of courtesies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> passed into Western literary tradition. The Ayyubid reconquest permanently reshaped the political geography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#levant">Levant</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusader-states">Crusader</a> coastal states that survived the Third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">Crusade</a> endured for another century but never again threatened Muslim dominance of the interior, and the balance of power established after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> set the course that led to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acre">Acre</a> in 1291. The dynasty's patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholarship through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madrasa">madrasa</a> foundations across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> strengthened the institutional foundations of Sunni learning in the region, and the Ayyubid period saw significant intellectual and artistic production, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a>, where the Citadel was rebuilt in its present form. The dynasty's greatest unintended legacy was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> that replaced it: the slave-soldier system on which the later Ayyubids relied produced an institution of exceptional military effectiveness that defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260, the first decisive check to Mongol expansion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a>, and governed Egypt and Syria until the Ottoman conquest of 1517.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut.jpg" alt="Battle of Ain Jalut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campaign_of_the_Battle_of_Ain_Jalut_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MapMaster, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire's decline was rooted in the governing decisions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a> made during his rise. By settling the empire's territories as autonomous principalities on his sons, brothers and nephews, notably in the arrangements of 1186, Saladin secured the loyalty needed to win at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, but ensured that his death would trigger a dynastic struggle capable of undoing much of what he had spent nearly two decades building. The succession conflict that erupted soon after Saladin's death in March 1193 was the first of many: his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> fought over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, and only the patient statesmanship of Saladin's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-adil">al-Adil</a> allowed the realm to be reunited under his authority by 1200. Al-Adil's capable reign (1200–1218) and that of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> (1218–1238) showed that a strong sultan could maintain Ayyubid coherence, but each succession brought renewed conflict. Al-Kamil's diplomatic cession of Jerusalem to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-ii">Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-jaffa-1229">Treaty of Jaffa (1229)</a>, giving up the city whose recovery had defined Saladin's reign in return for support against his own Ayyubid relatives, was the ideological nadir of the dynasty, showing how far the founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jihad">jihad</a> ideal had been subordinated to dynastic survival. The loss of Egypt to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluks</a> in 1250, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#turanshah">Turanshah</a> was murdered by his father's own Bahri mamluks, removed the economic foundation of the entire system. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongol-invasion-of-the-levant">Mongol invasion of the Levant</a> in 1259–1260, which followed the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a> and ended the principalities of Aleppo and Damascus under al-Nasir Yusuf, extinguished most of what remained, although Hama survived as a Mamluk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> into the fourteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a> was fought and won by the Mamluks, not the Ayyubids, demonstrating which institution had proved the more durable product of the dynasty's military system.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ayyubid Empire's brief, brilliant arc, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a>'s unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusader-states">Crusaders</a> to the dynasty's displacement by the very mamluk soldiers its later sultans had relied upon, encapsulates one of history's most poignant lessons: that a state built on the genius of a single founder and on the generous distribution of power among kinsmen may be undone by those same kinsmen the moment the founder's hand is removed.</em></p>

